4. Single most important security weakness in the authentication implementation
Unauthenticated password reset with no proof of ownership — src/app/api/auth/forgot-password/route.ts, lines 27–37 (context: 17–25, 39–44)
Evidence from source:

Line 7: export async function POST — no session or authentication is required.
Line 17: findUserByLogin(login) resolves the target account from email/username supplied by the caller.
Lines 27–37: if newPassword is present, line 31 hashes it and line 32 executes db.update(users).set({ passwordHash, ... }).where(eq(users.id, user.id)), then returns success (lines 33–36).
Lines 19–25 and 39–44: a mockToken generated with Math.random() is returned to the caller. Grep across src/ shows no other file reads, stores, or validates this token — there is no token table, no expiry, no verification step between identification and the password change.
No session requirement, no rate limiting, no delivery channel (no mail/queue dependency exists in package.json).
Why it matters: any anonymous caller who knows a username or e-mail can set that account's password and then log in normally through /auth/login. It bypasses the entire login control, including for privileged accounts (the seed creates an admin user at src/db/seed.ts:205-215).

Confirmed: all code behavior above, and the absence of any token validation anywhere in src/. Assumption: absence of rate limiting is inferred from the absence of any limiter in the repo (Next.js provides none by default); an external gateway/WAF layer cannot be ruled out from source alone.

Runner-up (stated for ranking only, not the answer): the unsigned, non-revocable raw-user-id session cookie at src/lib/auth.ts:58 (value re-parsed at line 23).

